\section{Persistent node heterogeneity and the initial condition}{
\code{node_sd > 0} gives each node a time-invariant effect \code{alpha_i} that
enters the outcome at every wave. Combined with a lagged outcome on the
right-hand side this is the \emph{dynamic panel} structure: \code{y_(i,t-1)} is a
function of \code{alpha_i} by construction, so ordinary least squares and
ordinary mixed-effects estimation are both inconsistent for \code{gamma}, and
the inconsistency propagates into \code{beta}. Generating data with
\code{node_sd > 0} is how that failure mode is measured rather than assumed;
rnetis itself provides no dynamic-panel estimator that removes it.

\code{initial_condition} controls how the wave-1 outcome relates to
\code{alpha_i}, which is not a detail -- it is the assumption dynamic-panel
estimators differ over:
\itemize{
\item \code{"stationary"} (default) starts the process at its closed-form AR(1)
equilibrium and then \strong{burns in} (see \code{burnin}) so that it reaches the
actual stationary distribution of the process being generated. The
closed form alone is not enough once exposure feeds the outcome back
through the network: it gets the mean and the marginal variance about
right and the \emph{cross-sectional covariance} wrong, and that difference is
precisely the collinearity between a node's lagged outcome and its
exposure to alters' lagged outcomes. This is the assumption that
system-GMM level moment conditions require (an estimator rnetis does not
provide).
\item \code{"independent"} draws \code{y_i1} independently of \code{alpha_i}, a deliberately
non-stationary start. Estimators that assume stationarity should be
expected to do worse here, and that contrast is the point.
}
}

\section{Process stability}{
When the exposure content is the outcome itself, iterating the model is a
vector autoregression with companion matrix \code{gamma * I + beta * W}, and
the generated series diverges unless that matrix's spectral radius is
below 1. With \code{normalize = "none"} the exposure term is a sum over alters,
so the radius grows roughly as \code{gamma + beta * mean_degree} -- at the
defaults with any appreciable density that is well above 1, and by the
fifth wave the outcome's variance is orders of magnitude larger than the
noise it was generated from. A single transition has nothing to iterate, so a
two-wave design does not show this.

The spectral radius is therefore computed from the realized networks, using
the exposure engine's own orientation (\code{W[i, j]} is the weight with which
\code{j} influences \code{i}; an undirected tie influences both endpoints), and
reported as a warning when it is at or above 1 and \code{n_waves > 2}, naming the
three ways out
(lower \code{beta}, \code{normalize = "row"}, or lower \code{density}). It is a warning
and not an error because an explosive process is a legitimate thing to
generate deliberately -- for studying near-unit-root behavior, say -- and
refusing it would make that region unreachable.
}

\section{Error dependence structure}{
\code{error_structure} selects what the disturbances do, which is a different
question from what the regressors are built from. A regressor can be
network-derived while the disturbances remain conditionally independent;
what breaks the independence assumptions of panel estimators is dependence
in the error itself.
\itemize{
\item \code{"independent"}: conditionally independent disturbances.
\item \code{"group_shock"}: a shared shock per (group, wave), so rows within a
group-wave are correlated.
\item \code{"network"}: a simultaneous-autoregressive disturbance process over the
generated network, \verb{eps = (I - error_rho * W)^-1 u} with \code{W} the
row-normalized influence matrix in the exposure engine's orientation,
giving residual network autocorrelation.
}
}

\section{What rnetis can fit, and what it only simulates}{

The simulator generates more designs than \code{\link[=fit_influence]{fit_influence()}} estimates. A
simulator's capability is not a widening of the estimator's scope.

\strong{Designs rnetis can fit.} One transition between two waves:
\code{n_waves = 2}, or any two waves of a longer simulation named with
\code{fit_influence(waves = c(from, to))}, which are then analyzed exactly as
two-wave data, with \code{y ~ ar() + exposure(y, via = tie)} (or
\code{exposure(x, via = tie)} for \code{exposure_content = "covariate"}). The
estimators that match the generating model are the two \code{\link[=fit_influence]{fit_influence()}}
resolves for such a formula: pooled least squares, and a mixed model when
the formula adds a group random intercept \code{(1 | group)} (the generating
model's \code{c_g} when \code{group_sd > 0}). Under the default settings
(\code{node_sd = 0}, \code{error_structure = "independent"}, \code{homophily = 0}) these
are fitted to the model that generated the data. The simulator generates
lagged exposure only, so the contemporaneous \code{lnam} route is not its
generating model.

\strong{Options that generate designs rnetis only simulates.} Each of these is
kept because it generates a design worth studying, but no rnetis estimator
models what it adds; fitting such data measures how the available
estimators behave when their model is wrong.
\itemize{
\item \code{n_waves > 2}: the multi-wave process is not modeled. Each fit
uses one transition; the others are present but unused.
\item \code{burnin}: a property of the generating process only (how close the
first recorded wave is to equilibrium).
\item \code{node_sd > 0}: a persistent node effect with a lagged outcome is a
dynamic panel, for which pooled and mixed estimates of \code{gamma} and
\code{beta} are inconsistent; rnetis has no dynamic-panel estimator.
\item \code{initial_condition}: matters only to dynamic-panel estimators,
which rnetis does not provide.
\item \code{error_structure = "group_shock"} or \code{"network"}: dependent
disturbances. No rnetis estimator models them; \code{\link[=netis_dependence]{netis_dependence()}}
measures the residual dependence they leave.
\item \code{homophily > 0}: tie formation that depends on the persistent node
effect, so the network is endogenous. rnetis's estimators condition on
the observed network.
\item \code{covariate_persistence} and \code{node_x_cor}: shape the covariate arm
(\code{exposure_content = "covariate"}) for dynamic-panel evaluation; with
\code{node_x_cor != 0} and \code{node_sd > 0} the covariate is correlated with
an omitted node effect, which no rnetis estimator removes.
}
